P-G3, spec 001 group 5 (W1; tasks.md T044, T049): the compose policy's host classes, for the Author's hand.

tools/gates/compose_policy.py is the gate's own tool, so its change is the Author's (tasks.md Format, "Patches"). Spec
001's tunnel adds two services to the one compose file, and the tunnel end's class two more, which spec 000's rules
refuse as written: only edge publishes, on 127.0.0.1; only the gateway joins egress; only edge the edge network; binds
from five roots; images from deploy/images.lock. This patch admits each, by name and exactly, and nothing else:
  - the laptop's `tunnel` joins egress, to dial the tunnel end, and binds ${H4_TUNNEL_DIR}, read-only;
  - the tunnel end's `tunnel-end` joins edge and publishes exactly 10.42.0.10:2222->2222 and 10.42.0.10:8087->2203,
    the public interface's fixed address, a literal no other host has, and binds /run/h4reet/tunnel-end/keys,
    read-only;
  - the tunnel end's collector joins egress, to reach EC2's inside the VPC;
  - a service in a cloud class's profile alone runs from the account's registry: a release-built image by the
    release's digest (${H4_DIGEST_<REPO>:-}), an upstream copy by the lock's own tag and digest; it never builds;
  - the tunnel end's env template is rendered too.
tools/gates/test/test_compose_policy.py gets a case for each, and its real-files fixture copies the tunnel's
Dockerfile. On an export: RED with the test half alone, 7 failed, 170 passed; GREEN with the whole patch, 177 passed.

Built from plain copies (git show bdb9808a:<path>), edited, then diff -u with a/ and b/ labels; never git apply. Apply
it on W1's -p branch with `patch -p1 < <this file>` at the repository's root. Until it is applied, the branch's policy
step refuses the tunnel's services (9 refusals), and four of its compose policy tests fail.
